{
  "id": "u02-l03",
  "unit": "u02-pronouns",
  "title": "tui, tumi, apni",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "Bengali has **three** words for 'you'. Think of them as a ladder of closeness:\n\n- **আপনি** (*apni*) — respect: elders, strangers, teachers, shopkeepers.\n- **তুমি** (*tumi*) — warmth: friends, younger people, partners, many parents and grandparents.\n- **তুই** (*tui*) — intimacy: your best friends of the same age, younger brothers and sisters, small children. Its verb ends in **-িস**: তুই কেমন **আছিস**? Plural: **তোরা**.\n\n**Careful:** তুই to a stranger or an elder is a serious insult. Quick scenarios: your grandmother → **আপনি** (some families use তুমি); your little brother → **তুই** or তুমি; a rickshaw driver → **আপনি**; your school best friend → **তুই**. When in doubt, go one step *more* polite.\n\n**এখানে** = here, **ওখানে** = there. The opposite of আছে is **নেই** (*nei*), and it never changes: রাফি এখানে **নেই** 'Rafi isn't here.'",
  "vocab": [
